# Question: Fermi style, how big is the market for a B2B product (TAM), the
# part we can serve (SAM) and the part we could win in three years (SOM), and
# how likely is the SOM to exceed 10M euros a year?
#
# Run it:
#   ubc --scenario product/tam-sam-som
# Change an assumption, for example a product that only serves one country:
#   ubc --set 'serviceable = 5% to 10%' --scenario product/tam-sam-som
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens som --scenario product/tam-sam-som
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Amounts are in euros a year.
# Fermi estimation: break the number into factors you can guess within a
# range; the errors partly cancel and the range of the result is honest.
#
# Sources: Douglas Hubbard, How to Measure Anything (2007), for Fermi
# decomposition; the company count comes from official statistics and the
# other ranges are typical experience.

# Assumptions
companies = 200k to 400k          # companies with 10 to 200 employees in the target countries; check official statistics
price = 3k to 8k                  # yearly price for one company; your price list or competitors' public ones
serviceable = 20% to 40%          # share we can serve (languages, integrations, sectors); typical experience
obtainable = 1% to 5%             # share of that we could win in three years against competitors; typical experience

# Calculation
tam = companies * price           # total addressable market: everyone buys
sam = tam * serviceable           # serviceable available market
som = sam * obtainable            # serviceable obtainable market

# Result
tam
sam
som
P(som > 10M)                      # how likely is the SOM to exceed 10M euros a year?

# Output (seed 1, 5000 samples):
#   tam = ≈ 1.38B   90% [762M … 2.49B]   98% [590M … 3.2B]   ▃▅▇██▇▆▄▄▃▂▂▂▁▁▁
#   sam = ≈ 389M   90% [198M … 779M]   98% [140M … 1.03B]   ▃▆██▇▆▅▄▄▃▂▂▂▁▁▁
#   som = ≈ 8.75M   90% [2.99M … 25.9M]   98% [1.8M … 40M]   ▅█▇▆▄▄▃▂▂▂▂▁▁▁▁▁
#   P(som > 10M) = 0.42
